    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.pairs\r\n\r\n### Question\r\n\r\nWhen I press `(`, both parentheses are inserted e.g.:\r\n```\r\n(|)\r\n```\r\nwhere `|` is the cursor.\r\n\r\nIn this position, when pressing enter, the typical result would be:\r\n```\r\n(\r\n    |\r\n)\r\n```\r\ndepending on your tab settings.\r\n\r\nBut in a `c` or `cpp` file, I'm seeing the following:\r\n```\r\n(\r\n |\r\n )\r\n```\r\nAn indent of only one space is performed and the closing parenthesis is also indented for some reason.\r\n\r\nIs there something with this plugin that is sensitive to filetype in this way? This only happens with `(`. Everything else seems to work as expected.\r\n\r\nHere is my `repro.lua` (stolen from lazy.nvim, beware this will create `.repro` directory):\r\n```lua\r\nvim.env.LAZY_STDPATH = \".repro\"\r\nload(\r\n  vim.fn.system(\r\n    \"curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/folke/lazy.nvim/main/bootstrap.lua\"\r\n  )\r\n)()\r\n\r\nrequire(\"lazy.minit\").repro({\r\n  spec = {\r\n    { \"nvim-mini/mini.pairs\", version = false, config = true },\r\n  },\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can try this using `nvim -u repro.lua test.lua` and comparing it with `nvim -u repro.lua test.c`",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.pairs</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Question</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">When I press <code class=\"notranslate\">(</code>, both parentheses are inserted e.g.:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"(|)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">(|)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">where <code class=\"notranslate\">|</code> is the cursor.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">In this position, when pressing enter, the typical result would be:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"(\n    |\n)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">(\n    |\n)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">depending on your tab settings.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">But in a <code class=\"notranslate\">c</code> or <code class=\"notranslate\">cpp</code> file, I'm seeing the following:</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"(\n |\n )\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">(\n |\n )\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">An indent of only one space is performed and the closing parenthesis is also indented for some reason.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Is there something with this plugin that is sensitive to filetype in this way? This only happens with <code class=\"notranslate\">(</code>. Everything else seems to work as expected.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Here is my <code class=\"notranslate\">repro.lua</code> (stolen from lazy.nvim, beware this will create <code class=\"notranslate\">.repro</code> directory):</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"vim.env.LAZY_STDPATH = &quot;.repro&quot;\nload(\n  vim.fn.system(\n    &quot;curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/folke/lazy.nvim/main/bootstrap.lua&quot;\n  )\n)()\n\nrequire(&quot;lazy.minit&quot;).repro({\n  spec = {\n    { &quot;nvim-mini/mini.pairs&quot;, version = false, config = true },\n  },\n})\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">env</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">LAZY_STDPATH</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span>.repro<span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span></span>\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">load</span>(\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">fn</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">system</span>(\n    <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span>curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/folke/lazy.nvim/main/bootstrap.lua<span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span></span>\n  )\n)()\n\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">require</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span>lazy.minit<span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span></span>).<span class=\"pl-c1\">repro</span>({\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">spec</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> {\n    { <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span>nvim-mini/mini.pairs<span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">version</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">false</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">config</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">true</span> },\n  },\n})</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">You can try this using <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim -u repro.lua test.lua</code> and comparing it with <code class=\"notranslate\">nvim -u repro.lua test.c</code></p>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.pairs\nQuestion\nWhen I press (, both parentheses are inserted e.g.:\n(|)\n\nwhere | is the cursor.\nIn this position, when pressing enter, the typical result would be:\n(\n    |\n)\n\ndepending on your tab settings.\nBut in a c or cpp file, I'm seeing the following:\n(\n |\n )\n\nAn indent of only one space is performed and the closing parenthesis is also indented for some reason.\nIs there something with this plugin that is sensitive to filetype in this way? This only happens with (. Everything else seems to work as expected.\nHere is my repro.lua (stolen from lazy.nvim, beware this will create .repro directory):\nvim.env.LAZY_STDPATH = \".repro\"\nload(\n  vim.fn.system(\n    \"curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/folke/lazy.nvim/main/bootstrap.lua\"\n  )\n)()\n\nrequire(\"lazy.minit\").repro({\n  spec = {\n    { \"nvim-mini/mini.pairs\", version = false, config = true },\n  },\n})\nYou can try this using nvim -u repro.lua test.lua and comparing it with nvim -u repro.lua test.c",
