            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> An indent of only one space is performed and the closing parenthesis is also indented for some reason.\r\n> \r\n> Is there something with this plugin that is sensitive to filetype in this way? This only happens with `(`. Everything else seems to work as expected.\r\n\r\nThere is nothing filetype dependent in 'mini.pairs' per se. *But* the `<CR>` implementation uses the approach that mimics the most robust approach that also respects filetype: pressing `<CR>` with 'mini.pairs' is basically the [same as pressing `<CR><C-o>O`](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/126ce3328c78399dcff58272f6f540a373b62a75/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L328-L330) without 'mini.pairs'. Here `<C-o>` temporarily exits into Normal mode and `O` inserts an empty line above (usually properly indented; at least with default value of 'indentkeys').\r\n\r\nI am afraid I can not help you with C filetype per se, but I can indeed reproduce this. The behavior with 'mini.pairs' is the same as `<C-o>O` without it. So my understanding is that is an intended default behavior which should be configurable with *some* options. I'd start looking in the direction of `:h 'cindent'`, `:h 'autoindent'`, `:h 'smartindent'`, etc. The C filetype does indeed have a long history in VIM of being treated as special, I am afraid.",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">An indent of only one space is performed and the closing parenthesis is also indented for some reason.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Is there something with this plugin that is sensitive to filetype in this way? This only happens with <code class=\"notranslate\">(</code>. Everything else seems to work as expected.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">There is nothing filetype dependent in 'mini.pairs' per se. <em>But</em> the <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;CR&gt;</code> implementation uses the approach that mimics the most robust approach that also respects filetype: pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;CR&gt;</code> with 'mini.pairs' is basically the <a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/126ce3328c78399dcff58272f6f540a373b62a75/doc/mini-pairs.txt#L328-L330\">same as pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;CR&gt;&lt;C-o&gt;O</code></a> without 'mini.pairs'. Here <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-o&gt;</code> temporarily exits into Normal mode and <code class=\"notranslate\">O</code> inserts an empty line above (usually properly indented; at least with default value of 'indentkeys').</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I am afraid I can not help you with C filetype per se, but I can indeed reproduce this. The behavior with 'mini.pairs' is the same as <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-o&gt;O</code> without it. So my understanding is that is an intended default behavior which should be configurable with <em>some</em> options. I'd start looking in the direction of <code class=\"notranslate\">:h 'cindent'</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">:h 'autoindent'</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">:h 'smartindent'</code>, etc. The C filetype does indeed have a long history in VIM of being treated as special, I am afraid.</p>",
            "bodyText": "An indent of only one space is performed and the closing parenthesis is also indented for some reason.\nIs there something with this plugin that is sensitive to filetype in this way? This only happens with (. Everything else seems to work as expected.\n\nThere is nothing filetype dependent in 'mini.pairs' per se. But the <CR> implementation uses the approach that mimics the most robust approach that also respects filetype: pressing <CR> with 'mini.pairs' is basically the same as pressing <CR><C-o>O without 'mini.pairs'. Here <C-o> temporarily exits into Normal mode and O inserts an empty line above (usually properly indented; at least with default value of 'indentkeys').\nI am afraid I can not help you with C filetype per se, but I can indeed reproduce this. The behavior with 'mini.pairs' is the same as <C-o>O without it. So my understanding is that is an intended default behavior which should be configurable with some options. I'd start looking in the direction of :h 'cindent', :h 'autoindent', :h 'smartindent', etc. The C filetype does indeed have a long history in VIM of being treated as special, I am afraid.",
